WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.479 --> 00:00:03.960
How long can you stay underwater?

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:10.929
It might depend on the size of your .... wait
for it...spleen.

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:13.180
Recent studies have revealed human adaptation

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.050
to the high altitudes of the Andes and Himalayas.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.460
These adaptations involve the ability of blood

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.439
to carry or hold oxygen in these low-oxygen

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:23.470
heights.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:25.760
Now researchers are looking for adaptations

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.970
in people who spend a lot of time under water.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.920
The Bajau people --also known as “Sea Nomads”--

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.190
have spent thousands of years diving deep

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.870
underwater without tanks or snorkels.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.780
Bajau divers may spend as much as 60 percent

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.270
of their workday under the surface, spearing

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.600
fish and harvesting sea cucumbers.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.680
mammals, including people have something called

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.649
a “dive reflex” -- which is triggered

00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:56.620
when you plunge your face into the water.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.440
Once submerged, your heart rate slows.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.080
Vessels in your extremities constrict-

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.960
shunting blood to the body’s core.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.480
And your spleen gets a big squeeze.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.760
This spleen squeeze pumps

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.110
oxygen-rich red blood cells into the bloodstream.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.900
And the researchers in this study found that

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.620
the Bajau have spleens that are 50 percent

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.170
larger than their land-based neighbors.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.700
It’s possible that these larger spleens

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.430
may serve as a reservoir for extra oxygenated

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.540
blood needed for long breathless dives.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.180
The researchers then looked to see

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.800
if there was a genetic component

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.840
they wanted to know if big spleens are inherited

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.380
or if something else was going on.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.840
After comparing the Bajou DNA with DNA from

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.810
nearby farmers and from Han Chinese, the team

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.500
found 25 genes that were different in the Bajou.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.280
One notable difference was in a gene called PDE10A.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.120
Other researchers had found that in mice,

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.030
this gene can affect thyroid activity, which

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.530
in turn affects spleen size.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.480
While it is unlikely one gene is responsible

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.670
for the size of the spleen, the research strongly

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.860
suggests that there is a genetic component

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.300
to this spleen enlargement.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.020
And it shows whether it’s high-altitude living

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.100
or deep-diving life,

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.540
humans have adapted to low-oxygen environments

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.520
in a number of different ways throughout history

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.460
I can’t wait to see what we do in space.

